1	\section{Results}
2	\label{sec:results}
3	
4	We organize results around three analyses: the binary ambiguity effect (C1), the type partial ordering (C2, including the inconclusive layer effect C3), and the model$\times$type interaction (C4). C1 and C2 are primary supported claims; C3 (layer effect) is inconclusive; C4 (model$\times$type interaction) is exploratory.
5	
6	\subsection{Binary Ambiguity Effect (C1)}
7	
8	\begin{figure}[t]
9	\centering
10	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_variance_decomposition.pdf}
11	\caption{Variance decomposition of violation rates (temperature $= 0$). Clause identity: 22.4\% (0.4\% between types); condition: 15.8\%; model: 1.0\%; residual: 60.8\%. Clause wording---not type membership---is the primary risk factor among measured predictors.}
12	\label{fig:variance_decomposition}
13	\end{figure}
14	
15	Across all five models and six types, ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 pp (41.0\% vs.\ 6.9\%, McNemar OR $= 15.1$ (based on $n = 580$ discordant pairs), 95\% CI $[10.78, 21.17]$, $p < 10^{-105}$; stratified Mantel--Haenszel OR $= 9.57$ $[7.63, 12.01]$; Appendix~\ref{app:mantel_haenszel}). All six types individually produce significant increases (all $p < 10^{-5}$) across both retail ($+$30.6\,pp) and airline ($+$37.7\,pp) domains (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_domain}), and all five models show large effects ($+25.7$ to $+39.0$ pp, all $p < 10^{-17}$). Perturbation controls confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity (\S\ref{sec:method}). Figure~\ref{fig:variance_decomposition} decomposes violation-rate variance\footnote{Type III sum of squares (SS); logistic regression (Appendix~\ref{app:regression}) confirms concordant results (Tjur's $R^2 = 0.175$; \citealp{tjur_2009}).}: clause identity explains 22.4\% (0.4\% between types, 22.0\% within), condition 15.8\%, model 1.0\%, and residual 60.8\% ($\sim$15\% stochastic, $\sim$46\% systematic; Appendix~\ref{app:residual}). No single clause drives any type-level effect (Appendix~\ref{app:clause_variance}), and surface features are weak clause-level predictors (Appendix~\ref{app:clause_predictors}). Excluding incompleteness ($+$49.2\,pp, subject to information-addition confound; Appendix~\ref{app:confound}), the effect is $+$31.0\,pp (95\% CI $[27.9, 34.1]$; Appendix~\ref{app:sans_incompleteness}). The confound-minimized coreferential type ($\Delta$token $\approx 7.8$ words) still produces $+$18.7\,pp, isolating the ambiguity-only contribution. $\Delta_{\text{token}}$ is a coarse proxy for information content; authorization scope exhibits similar token differences ($\Delta = 36.1$ words; Appendix~\ref{app:clause_length}) without the information-addition confound. A logistic regression controlling for per-clause token-count differences confirms the effect persists at $\Delta_{\text{token}} = 0$ ($+$20.5\,pp, 95\% CI $[14.2, 26.8]$; Appendix~\ref{app:token_sensitivity}).
16	
17	\begin{figure}[t]
18	\centering
19	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_heatmap.pdf}
20	\caption{Violation rates (\%) by model and type under ambiguous conditions. Authorization scope varies 52\,pp across models ($\kappa = 0.385$), reflecting differential safety training; incompleteness remains uniformly high (46--62\%).}
21	\label{fig:heatmap}
22	\end{figure}
23	
24	Convergent evidence from two additional models confirms the effect across seven models ($\Delta = +20.0$ to $+33.6$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}); cross-benchmark evaluation on SOPBench~\citep{li_sopbench_2025} shows directional consistency ($+12.7$\,pp unweighted mean; Appendix~\ref{app:sopbench}). Figure~\ref{fig:cross_benchmark} summarizes generalization across benchmarks. The taxonomy predicts failure mechanisms more strongly than violation rate (NMI $= 0.23$ vs.\ $0.01$; $V = 0.45$ vs.\ $0.14$ on tables of different dimensionality; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}), making type-level analysis (C2--C4) informative for diagnosis.
25	
26	\begin{figure}[t]
27	\centering
28	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_cross_benchmark.pdf}
29	\caption{Cross-benchmark generalization. The ambiguity effect replicates across benchmarks ($n = 19$ model--benchmark data points). Diamonds: pooled effect with 95\% CIs; circles: per-model effects showing cross-model spread (Appendix~\ref{app:sopbench}, \ref{app:healthcare}, \ref{app:model_expansion}).}
30	\label{fig:cross_benchmark}
31	\end{figure}
32	
33	\subsection{Type Partial Ordering (C2)}
34	
