\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Disambiguation neglect and prompt-level safety.}
The 4.6\% awareness rate implies prompt-level safety instructions are insufficient; authorization scope responds to explicit disambiguation ($-$18.0\,pp), but incompleteness does not ($-$3.4\,pp, n.s.; Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}). Convergent nocuity (\S\ref{sec:nocuity}) further undermines agreement-based monitoring; cross-family Jaccard similarity approaching within-family levels suggests shared default-interpretation heuristics rather than training-data overlap as the convergence mechanism. The type--mechanism dissociation maps types to targeted mitigations---numerical thresholds for authorization scope, structural disambiguation for scopal, and default-value specification for incompleteness---but the variance decomposition (22.4\% clause vs.\ 0.4\% type) means detection must operate at the clause level, with type labels guiding the form of disambiguation once a clause is flagged. A proof-of-concept classifier achieves binary detection F1\,=\,0.81 (Appendix~\ref{app:detection}), suggesting such automated clause-level screening is feasible.

\paragraph{Conclusion.}
This work provides the first causal evidence that policy ambiguity induces safety violations in LLM agents, replicating across benchmarks and model families. Convergent nocuity inverts classical ambiguity theory \citep{berry_kamsties_2004}, defeating agreement-based monitoring; clause-level auditing outperforms type-level screening (22.4\% vs.\ 0.4\% variance).

